Z||000021||Tina||05-22-2001||05:55 PM||||Wow, you leave the computer for a couple of days,......gone sandblasting by suspension.<P>Thanks, guys.<BR>And yes, got that faded black on the inside of the lenses as well.<BR>You would think if you did a really good job taping off the clear you could paint the inside with flat black.<BR>Gonna have to wait till the Foliatec stuff comes in the mail, to prove my theory.<P>BTW mrfixit58, how often do I have to repeat this procedure? <BR>  <P>See ya, off to blast the tranny  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||mrfixit58||05-23-2001||08:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>BTW mrfixit58, how often do I have to repeat this procedure?  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If your referring to the clean and polish procedure all I can say is, "whenever the get dull". I polished mine the week before Daytona. I drive my car almost everyday and park it where the sun sines on the back of the car all day. The lenses STILL look like new. For $15 and a few minutes worth of work, it's a great deal.<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||14
Z||000023||rogergarrison||05-23-2001||09:48 AM||||Lisa painted hers. It would def be better to use transparent colors like candies and pearls. Basecoat colors are pretty non-transparent, almost covers like primer. Most motorcycle colors are candies or variations.<P>If your just talking about making the orig shiny. Ive just wet sanded with #1000 and rubbed it out with a buffer and rubbing compound, then polish. Ive redone lots of vans with plastic picture windows that you couldnt even see thru, they were so oxidized.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
